===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=skeletal joint|:inh|enm:ancle|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|ankel}}, {{m|enm|ancle}}, {{m|enm|ankyll}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ancol}} (compare {{m|ang|anclēow|t=ankle}} > Modern {{cog|en|anclef}}, {{m|en|ancliff}}, {{m|en|ancley}}), from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*ankul}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ankulaz|t=ankle}}; akin to {{cog|is|ökkla}}, {{m|is|ökli}}, {{cog|da,sv|ankel}}, {{cog|nl|enkel}}, {{m|nl|enklaauw}}, {{cog|de|Enkel}}, {{cog|non|akka}}, {{cog|ofs|anckel}}, and perhaps {{cog|goh|encha}}, {{m|goh|ancha|t=thigh, shin}}, from the {{cog|gem-pro|*ankijǭ|t=ankle, joint}}.

Compare with {{cog|sa|अङ्ग||limb}}, {{m|sa|अङ्गुरि||finger}}, {{cog|la|angulus}}. Compare {{m|en|haunch}} and Greek prefix {{m|grc|ἀγκυλο-||joint, crooked, bent}}. {{doublet|en|angulus|angle}}.

