{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|metallurgy}} To [[subject#Verb|subject]] to great [[heat]] and then (often [[slow]]) [[cooling]], and sometimes [[reheat]]ing and further cooling, for the purpose of [[render#Verb|rendering]] less [[brittle]]; to [[temper]]; to [[toughen]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=O. E.|title=The Process of Making Iron Wire|editor=[[w:Hezekiah Niles|H[ezekiah] Niles]]|magazine=[[w:Weekly Register|The Weekly Register]]|location=Baltimore, Md.|publisher=Printed and published by the editor, at the Franklin Press, South-Street, next door to the Merchants' Coffee-House|date=7 March 1812|volume=II|issue=27|page=9|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LZE-AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA9|oclc=472971892|passage=The best tough soft iron, such as will weld round, is drawn into rods by smiths, using charcoal fires, and taking welding heats every time, the rod is about 1.4 inch diameter, 9 or 10 feet long, containing 4lbs. each, tapered at each end to a long point; they are first '''anealed''' by being brought to a bright red heat, in a furnace excluding the air as much as possible, for if the air can be entirely excluded, no scab will rise in '''anealing'''; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=C. Barus; [[w:Vincenc Strouhal|V[incenc] Strouhal]]|chapter=The Effect of Sudden Cooling Exhibited by Glass and by Steel, Considered both Physically and Chemically|title=Report of Work Done in the Division of Chemistry and Physics Mainly During the Fiscal Year 1885–'86|series=Bulletins of the {{w|United States Geological Survey}}|seriesvolume=no. 42; 50th [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], 2d session, [[w:United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] Miscellaneous Documents; no. 137|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|Government Printing Office]]|year=1887|year_published=1889|volume=VII|page=123|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=M-mbQTOQmRkC&pg=PA123|oclc=881832145|passage=On closer inspection it appeared that steel '''annealed''' at 100° is, cæteris paribus, more easily soluble than glass hard steel; steel '''annealed''' at 200° more easily soluble than steel '''annealed''' at 100°, and steel '''annealed''' at 360° more easily soluble than steel '''annealed''' at 200°. {{...}} These curious results were substantiated by '''annealing''' one-half of short glass hard rods (ca. 5<sup>cm</sup> in length) at red heat on dissolving in HCl, the diameter of the soft length is diminished more rapidly than the diameter of the hard length.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James W[alter] Mayer; Lennart Eriksson; John A[rthur] Davies|chapter=General Features of Ion Implantation|title=Ion Implantation in Semiconductors: Silicon and Germanium|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Academic Press|year=1970|page=5|oclc=912401876|passage=The isolated disordered regions and the amorphous layer have widely different '''anneal''' behavior. In the case of germanium and silicon, the isolated disordered regions '''anneal''' at moderate temperatures of approximately 200° and 300° C, respectively. The amorphous layers also '''anneal''' in a characteristic fashion, but at appreciably higher temperatures, i.e., at approximately 600° C in silicon and 400° C in germanium. It should be noted that even though both types of disorder can be '''annealed''' at temperatures well below those where diffusion of the dopant species occurs, there are still defects present.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1979
|author=w:J.G. Ballard
|title=w:The Unlimited Dream Company
|section=chapter 30
|passage= I felt his strong bones '''anneal''' themselves to my own, his blood vent its bright tide into my veins, the semen of his testicles foam as it dashed in a torrent against mine.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kate McKinnon|title=Sculptural Metal Clay Jewelry: Techniques and Explorations|location=Loveland, Colo.|publisher=Interweave Press|year=2010|page=27|isbn=978-1-59668-174-3|passage=A properly made, fully sintered and fully '''annealed''' metal clay piece should be able to stand up to any traditional metalsmithing technique.}}
# To [[cool]] [[glass]] slowly, to minimize internal [[stress]].
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[burn]] [[color]]s onto a [[glass]] or other surface.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Potter (bishop)|John Potter]]|chapter=Of the Parts, Ornaments, &c. of Ships|title=Archæologia Græca: Or, The Antiquities of Greece|edition=2nd augmented and improved|location=London|publisher=Printed for S[amuel] and J[ohn] Sprint, at the Bell; and John Nicholson, at the King's-Arms, in [[w:Little Britain, London|Little-Britain]]; and for Timothy Child, at the White-Hart, in [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|St. Paul's Church-Yard]]|year=1706|volume=II (Containing I. The Military Affairs of the Grecians. II. Some of Their Miscellany Customs)|page=127|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xN5eAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA127|oclc=10036528|passage=It was customary to beautify the Prow with Gold, and various ſorts of Paint and Colours: {{...}} Several other Colours were alſo made uſe of, nor were they barely varniſh'd over with them, but very often '''anneal'd''' by Wax melted in the Fire, ſo as neither the Sun, Winds, or Water were able to deface them.}}
<!-- actually a Herbert quote, can be reinserted if correctly attributed #* {{quote-book|author=Gene Edward Veith, Jr.|chapter=The Word of God: Revelation and Proclamation|title=Reformation Spirituality: The Religion of {{w|George Herbert}}|location=Eugene, Or.|publisher={{w|Wipf and Stock}}|year=2013|page=204|isbn=978-1-62032-830-9|passage=As on a window late I cast mine eye, / I saw a vine drop grapes with ''J'' and ''C'' / '''Anneal'd''' on every bunch. One standing by / Ask'd what it meant. {{...}}}}-->
