## {{senseid|en|repository}} A [[repository]] or other [[place#Noun|place]] for [[store#Verb|storing]] [[material#Noun|material]] (such as [[document#Noun|documents]] and [[record#Noun|records]]) no longer in [[current#Adjective|current]] [[use#Noun|use]], and usually having [[historical#Adjective|historical]] [[value#Noun|value]].
##: {{coord|en|gallery|library|museum}}
##* {{RQ:Howell Epistolae|date=20 October 1632|chapter=III. To the Right Honourable the Earl R. from Hamburgh.|page=222|passage=The chiefeſt of the firſt Precinct vvas ''Lubeck'', vvhere the '''Archives''' of their ancient Records, and their prime Chancery is ſtill, {{...}}|footer=Spelled ''Archifs'' in the 1st edition.}}
##* {{RQ:Sterne Sentimental Journey|volume=II|chapter=The Sword. Rennes.|page=56|passage=The preſident accepted the Marquis's ſvvord—he ſtay'd a fevv minutes to ſee it depoſited in the '''archives''' of his houſe—and departed.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1851|author=w:Adolfo de Castro|chapter=XXI|translator=Thomas Parker|title=The Spanish Protestants, and Their Persecution by [[w:Philip II of Spain|Philip II.]]; a Historical Work,{{nb...|by Señor Don Adolfo de Castro. Translated, from the Original Spanish, by Thomas Parker.}}|location=London|publisher=Charles Gilpin,{{nb...|Bishopsgate Street without.}}; Edinburgh: [[w:A & C Black|Adam and Charles Black]]; Dublin: J. B. Gilpin|page=324|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/spanishprotestan00cast/page/324/mode/1up|oclc=1808893|passage=The court, however, was relieved from giving judgment on the case before it, by the death of the prince, which put a stop to its proceedings; the minutes of which were sealed up in a green coffer, and, by order of the king, deposited in the '''archive''' of Simancas by Christóval de Mora.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=20 September 2017|author=James Somers|title=Keepers of the Secrets|journal=w:The Village Voice|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260509121346/https://www.villagevoice.com/keepers-of-the-secrets/|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Village Voice|issn=0042-6180|oclc=1696813|passage="I don't know how one could be interested in libraries and ''not'' '''archives'''," {{quote-gloss|Thomas}} Lannon told me. They tell you "the stories ''behind'' things," he said, "the unpublished, the hard to find, the true story."}}
## The material so stored, [[consider]]ed [[as a whole]].
##: {{ux|en|His '''archive''' of Old High German texts is the most extensive in Britain.}}
##* {{RQ:Plutarch Lives|volume=I|chapter=Plutarch|pages=22–23|pageref=23|passage=[H]e took a particular Journy to ''Sparta'', to ſearch the '''Archives''' of that famous Commonvvealth, to underſtand throughly the model of their ancient Government, their Legiſlators, their Kings, and their ''Ephori'', {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Lamb Essays of Elia|page=21|passage={{...}} I found {{quote-gloss|G. D.}} busy as a moth over some rotten '''archive''', rummaged out of some seldom-explored press, in a nook at Oriel.}}
### {{lb|en|by extension|ecology}} A [[natural#Adjective|natural]] [[deposit#Noun|deposit]] of material, [[regard#Verb|regarded]] as a record of [[environmental#Adjective|environmental]] [[change#Noun|changes]] [[over time]].
###: {{coi|en|peat '''archive'''&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; soil '''archive'''}}
###* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=w:Dag Olav Hessen|chapter=Between a Snowball and Hell|translator=Kerri Pierce|title=The Many Lives of Carbon|location=London|publisher=w:Reaktion Books|section=part II (The C in Cycle)|page=178|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/manylivesofcarbo0000hess/page/178/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-78023-851-7|passage=Even these 100,000-year cycles seem to be subject to still longer fluctuations, but here the historical '''archive''' begins to be less reliable. The ice '''archive''', in any case, can no longer help us (ice clearly does not preserve prehistorical temperatures directly, but rather atmospheric isotope signals, which are just as dependable).}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[collection]], a repository.
#* {{RQ:Plutarch Holland Morals|chapter=Of Cvriositie|page=140|passage=[T]heſe curious meddlers in collecting and gathering together on all ſides the errours, imperfections, defaults, and ſolæciſmes (as I may ſo ſay) not of verſes or Poemes, but of other mens lives, make of their memorie a moſt unpleaſant '''Archive''' or Regiſter, and uncivile Record, vvhich they ever carie about them.}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle Friedrich|volume=III|chapter=The Homaging|page=53|passage=[S]o expert was he, and a living '''archive''' in that business: however, his post seems to have vanished with himself.}}

