# To [[create]] based on [[theory]] or [[general knowledge]] rather than [[data]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1966 |title=Sales Management - Volume 97, Issues 8-14 |author=|page=31 |ISBN=|passage=Research for program's subject matter is like mining gold. The more raw material we have, the more likely we are going to find gold nuggets. But this step is often overlooked and a program is "'''armchaired'''" from the office of the vice-president or vice-president of sales.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1970|title=The national assessment approach to exercise development |author=Carmen J. Finley; Frances S. Berdie |page=84 |ISBN=|passage=The very serious question is then raised as to whether reasonable and logical distractors can be "'''armchaired'''" or whether the practice of administering a question in open-end format to obtain logical distractors is a better procedure.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1976 |title=A couple's guide to communication |author=John Mordechai Gottman |page= xxv|ISBN=|passage=We think it makes sense to generate interventions empirically by finding out how couples deal with conflict, rather than by '''armchairing''' interventions.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=27 May 14|title=On Types Of Typology|work=Slate Star Codex|url=https://slatestarcodex.com/2014/05/27/on-types-of-typologies/|author=Scott Alexander|text=MBTI has four different variables, each of which has two possible values. This could be interpreted as an implicit claim that there are four dimensions to personality, and only two types of personality within each dimension. And Myers and Briggs had no basis on which to make such a claim and just sort of '''armchaired''' it. Interpreted that way, the theory is pseudoscientific and dumb.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260722165332/https://slatestarcodex.com/2014/05/27/on-types-of-typologies/|archivedate=22 July 2026}}
# To [[theorize]] based on [[analysis]] of data that was gathered previously; to [[reflect]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1968|title=Cooperative extension organization and administration|author=Daniel C. Pfannstiel; Barbara H. Matthews |page= |ISBN=|passage=In past years, we administered this questionnaire and gave the results to the president who sat at a conference table with top management and '''armchaired''' some answers. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1976|title=Advances in Psychological and Educational Measurement |author=Dato N De Gruijter; Leo J. Th. van der Kamp |page=113 |ISBN=|passage=Briefly it may be stated: Operations come first; concepts follow; theory  aims at developing concepts, from operations, plus a nomological network for those concepts, which explains the structure of the data obtained through those operations. And this does not exclude the theorist from doing some ''''armchairing'''' in thinking about logically consistent models, their empirical potentialities, their assumptions and their implications; he may, and usually will, venture some possible empirical interpretations of a model, but in doing so he will carefully avoid any substantive (nonformal) pre-operational definition of a concept or construct.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|title=Deadly Feasts|author=Richard Rhodes |page= |ISBN=|passage=Even before the Glasses had arrived in New Guinea, two American anthropologists at Tulane University, Ann and J. L. Fischer, had '''armchaired''' a connection between kuru and cannibalism by working their way through the findings of a team of anthropologists who had studied the Fore in the early 1950s, Ronald and Catherine Berndt, as well as the many papers on kuru that Gajdusek, Zigas and various Australian investigators had published.}}

